# =============================================================================
# Eventful — frontend environment
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env.local`. Only PUBLIC, client-safe values live here: everything
# prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is inlined into the JavaScript bundle and visible to
# anyone who opens devtools. No backend secret ever belongs in this file.
#
# These SEVEN are the only variables the application reads. Each was verified
# against actual `process.env` usage in the source; the full reference is
# ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLES.md at the repo root.
# =============================================================================


##################################################
# API + site URLs  (REQUIRED)
##################################################
# Both are REQUIRED for a production build — `next build` FAILS without them,
# by design (lib/api/config.ts).
#
# They used to fall back to localhost, which is right in development and
# catastrophic in production while being silent in both: a build with them
# unset emitted a sitemap, canonical tags and OpenGraph URLs pointing at
# localhost — which search engines index exactly as written — and every
# server-rendered page called an API on the container's own loopback.

# Backend REST API origin, no trailing slash. Read by the typed API client, the
# `gen:api` type generation, and the push service worker (app/sw.js/route.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# This site's own public origin. Canonical URLs, sitemap, OpenGraph.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000


##################################################
# Payments  (OPTIONAL)
##################################################
# Razorpay Checkout's PUBLIC key id — client-safe by design.
#
# OPTIONAL because `POST /bookings` returns the key its order was created with,
# and THAT one always wins. Set this so a hard refresh on the payment page can
# still open Checkout, and for a frontend pointed at a backend that does not
# send one.
#
# Never put the Razorpay SECRET here — it signs webhooks and belongs only on
# the backend.
NEXT_PUBLIC_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=


##################################################
# Media host  (OPTIONAL)
##################################################
# Where uploaded posters and photos are actually served from, when they are
# served from a bucket or CDN rather than through the API
# (STORAGE_BACKEND=s3 or gcs on the backend — mirror S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL here).
#
# `next/image` refuses to optimise any host not on its allow-list, and
# next.config.mjs now builds that list from NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL and this
# variable. Leave blank while the backend serves /media/ itself.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL=


##################################################
# Sign-in providers  (FUTURE INTEGRATION — leave unset)
##################################################
# Both are OFF by default and the UI says so plainly rather than pretending:
# the Google, Apple and phone buttons render, and pressing one fails instantly
# with a sentence naming the provider — never a spinner, never a success that
# did not happen. An auth control that appears to work is the worst thing to
# fake, because a ticket and a payment are attributed to whoever it claims
# you are.
#
# DO NOT SET EITHER UNTIL THE BACKEND ENDPOINTS EXIST. Setting one turns the
# buttons live, and they would then fail against a 404 instead of explaining
# themselves.

# Turns on the Google/Apple buttons, which navigate to
# `<base>/<provider>/start?next=<return path>`. Needs the Django OAuth
# endpoints — see REAL_INTEGRATIONS_AUDIT.md (R1/R2) and the backend's
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_* block. NOT Supabase Auth; this project uses its own JWTs.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_OAUTH_BASE_URL=/api/v1/auth/oauth

# Turns on phone + OTP sign-in. Needs POST /auth/otp/request and
# POST /auth/otp/verify (R3). The SMS template and the `otp` throttle scope
# already exist on the backend.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PHONE_AUTH_ENABLED=1


##################################################
# Google Maps  (OPTIONAL)
##################################################
# The Maps JavaScript API key, for the interactive map on an event page and
# the venue picker's map preview. It CANNOT be proxied — the map renders in
# the browser, so the browser needs a key.
#
# SAME KEY VALUE as the backend's GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY is fine and is what the
# one-key model expects. Restrict it in the Google Cloud console by HTTP
# referrer (your domains) and to the Maps JavaScript API. That restriction is
# the only protection a browser key can have: the value itself is public by
# necessity, because anything the browser uses is in the page source.
#
# Everything else — Places, Geocoding, Directions, Distance Matrix, Photos —
# goes through the BACKEND on the server key, so those never appear here.
#
# Blank means no interactive map. The event page then renders the address and
# a plain Google Maps directions link, which answers "how do I get there"
# completely and costs nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=


##################################################
# Google Calendar — nothing to configure here
##################################################
# Deliberately absent. The OAuth client id and secret live only on the
# backend; the frontend asks `GET /api/v1/me/integrations/google` whether
# calendar sync is available and connected, then sends the browser to an
# authorization URL the backend mints. A client secret in this file would be
# inlined into the bundle and readable by every visitor.


##################################################
# Sentry  (CREDENTIAL HELD — SDK NOT WIRED YET)
##################################################
# This is the FRONTEND DSN — a different Sentry project from the backend's.
# Using one DSN for both merges server and browser errors into a single stream
# where you can no longer tell which side broke.
#
# NOTHING READS THIS YET. The frontend has no Sentry SDK: browser errors
# currently surface only in the user's own console. Setting this alone changes
# nothing — it needs `@sentry/nextjs` installed and its client/server/edge
# config files added, which is a small piece of real work, not a config
# toggle. Ask and it can be wired.
#
# Sentry → your Next.js project → Settings → Client Keys (DSN).
#
# A frontend DSN is PUBLIC by design — it is embedded in the bundle of every
# Sentry-instrumented site on the internet. It is an ingest endpoint, not a
# credential: it can send events to your project and read nothing. That is why
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is correct here and would be catastrophic for the backend one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=


##################################################
# Web Push — nothing to configure here
##################################################
# Deliberately absent. The frontend asks the BACKEND whether push is available
# (`GET /api/v1/push/config`) and receives the VAPID public key from it, so the
# key lives in exactly one place and cannot drift between the two apps.
#
# Push stays off until the backend has VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY / VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY /
# VAPID_CONTACT set. While it is off the UI renders nothing for it rather than
# asking for a browser permission it could never honour.


##################################################
# Dev tooling — not application config
##################################################
# `npm run mock:api` (scripts/mock-api.mjs) reads MOCK_API_PORT,
# MOCK_API_ORIGIN and MOCK_RAZORPAY_KEY_ID; Playwright reads CI. All have
# working defaults and none is read by the application, so they are documented
# here rather than declared — putting them in .env.local would imply the app
# uses them.


##################################################
# Brand, contact and social — the "refuse rather than pretend" block
##################################################
# Every value here is OPTIONAL, and every one of them renders NOTHING when
# unset rather than rendering a plausible-looking placeholder. That is the
# point of the block: the footer used to hard-code four social links to
# `https://instagram.com`, `https://x.com`, `https://facebook.com` and
# `https://youtube.com` — the platforms' own front doors, not accounts. A
# visitor who clicked one landed on a login wall, which reads as a broken
# product rather than as an absent one.
#
# So: set a handle and the icon appears. Leave it blank and the icon is
# filtered out. Leave them ALL blank and the social row is absent entirely and
# the payment pills take the closing band on their own. Same rule the push
# card, the OAuth buttons and the operator console's health tiles follow.

# Full URLs, not handles — they are used verbatim as hrefs.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_INSTAGRAM=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_X=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_FACEBOOK=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_YOUTUBE=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SOCIAL_LINKEDIN=

# ── Support channels, shown on /contact ──────────────────────────────────
# /contact lists five channels (booking help, phone, organizer enquiries,
# press, security disclosure) and routes them all through these two values
# with a per-channel `mailto:` subject. An unset one renders "Not open yet"
# instead of an address that bounces — a support address that bounces converts
# somebody who needed help into somebody who thinks they were ignored.
#
# With NEITHER set, /contact renders an explicit "support channels are being
# set up" notice. The page still exists and is still linked, because it is one
# of the four pages an Indian payment gateway checks for during merchant
# onboarding.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_PHONE=

# ── Company registration details, shown on /terms and /privacy ───────────
# The governing-law clause on /terms prints the registered address when this is
# set, and otherwise states plainly that the entity is being registered and
# points at /contact. An INVENTED address on a Terms page is exactly the detail
# that voids the document it appears on, so there is no placeholder to
# accidentally ship.
#
# GSTIN is not rendered anywhere yet — it is declared here because the tax
# invoice (PENDING_TASKS 4.2.3 / 10.2) is the next thing that needs it, and a
# GST-registered platform must show it on one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_REGISTERED_ADDRESS=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GSTIN=

# NOTE: the platform fee is deliberately NOT here. It is a flat per-ticket
# amount in paise, owned by the backend's PLATFORM_FEE_PER_TICKET and mirrored
# once in `lib/booking/selection.ts`, which both the checkout AND /pricing
# import. A second copy in env is how the pricing page and the payment screen
# end up quoting different numbers for the same fee.
